/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  wineAcids01_tartaric_malic -- the three main organic acids of a wine together, titrated with
  potassium hydroxide: 0.03 mol/kg tartaric acid + 0.03 mol/kg malic acid + 0.03 mol/kg lactic
  acid in water, with 0 to 0.18 mol/kg KOH.  The titratable protons are 0.03 x (2 + 2 + 1) =
  0.15 mol/kg, so the axis runs from 0 to 1.2 equivalents of acid; ten beakers.  pH and the
  distribution of the eight species H2Tart / HTart- / Tart2- / H2Malic / HMalic- / Malic2- /
  HLac / Lac-.  (The file keeps the name given when it held two acids.)

  ILLUSTRATIVE COMPOSITION.  No sourced must or wine composition is in hand, so 0.03 mol/kg
  of each is a round declared number, in the order of magnitude of the acids of a wine (a few
  g/L each; lactic acid is the product of the malolactic fermentation and rarely sits beside
  this much malic acid); the case says nothing about any particular wine, and there is no
  ethanol, no potassium or calcium of the wine, no citric or acetic acid, no ion pairing.  It
  is a structural illustration of three families on one pH axis, NOT validated against a
  measured pH: no source in hand prints one, so no `anchor` rows.

  CONSTANTS (25 C, I = 0).  Tartaric: pK1 3.036, pK2 4.366, dH +3.19 / +0.93 kJ/mol,
  Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen 2002 Table 7.63 p. 347 (agreeing with Patterson & Woolley 2001
  Tables 9-10).  Malic: pK1 3.459, pK2 5.097, dH +2.955 / -1.181 kJ/mol of the d,l acid,
  Eden & Bates 1959 (J. Res. Natl. Bur. Stand. 62, 161) Table 6 p. 169.  Lactic: pK 3.862,
  dH -0.251 kJ/mol, Nims & Smith 1936 (J. Biol. Chem. 113, 145) Table II p. 148.

  The pH is a RESULT: `pH solve;` closes the charge balance and `totalsBasis stoichiometric`
  makes the potassium a titrant, as in bjerrum01_carbonate_pH.

  WHAT TO LOOK FOR.  (this run's numbers, Davies, 25 C; molalities in mmol/kg of each acid's 30)
   1. The three acids alone (KOH = 0): pH 2.210.  The strongest acid is the most ionised:
      tartaric 4.2 + 0.04 of 30 (HTart + Tart), malic 1.7, lactic 0.71 (Lac).  The order of
      the acids is the order of pK1: tartaric 3.036, malic 3.459, lactic 3.862.
   2. At KOH = 0.06 (pH 3.528, 0.4 equivalents) lactate is 11.0, HMalic 17.3 and HTart 19.5:
      the three acids are ionised in the order of their pK's, at one common pH.
   3. At KOH = 0.10 (pH 4.163) Tart2- is 16.9 against HTart- 12.3: the second step of
      tartaric acid (pK2 4.366) is half done only a little above here, while lactate is
      already 21.7 of 30 and Malic2- 5.4 (pK2 5.097).
   4. At KOH = 0.14 (pH 5.220) lactate is 29.0, Tart2- 28.3, Malic2- 22.7 and HMalic- 7.2:
      malic acid, with the highest pK2, is the last to finish.
   5. At KOH = 0.15 (the titratable protons 0.03 x 5) all three are fully ionised
      (>= 29.99 of 30), pH 8.587; the next beaker has 0.03 mol/kg free KOH and pH 12.348.
   The curve has no plateau at any single pK: five proton steps lie between pH 3.0 and 5.1,
   so the solution is buffered across the whole of that range, which is the point of a
   wine's acid mixture.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

